  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nFriday into Saturday, the upper flow will become southwesterly as an \nupper trough and closed low across the Pacific Northwest deepens \ninto the Western U.S. Surface low pressure will persist across the \nNorthern and Central High Plains, bringing a breezy southerly winds \n15 to 25 mph by Friday afternoon, and highs 85 to near 90. Some\nguidance supports highs could reach the lower 90s across much \nof the west. The record high for North Platte is 95 degrees. \nRecords for Imperial, Broken Bow and Valentine are 91 degrees \nFriday. Based on current forecast trends, Imperial and Broken \nBow have the best chances of hitting a record high on \nFriday. Southerly winds will increase to 15 to 25 mph Friday \nafternoon across the central and east. Elevated fire weather \nconditions west of Valentine through Imperial, where afternoon \nhumidity could reach the upper teens and winds gust up to 20 \nmph. Southerly winds will be even stronger from 15 to 35 mph \nFriday night. This mixing will also keep low very mild from the \nupper 50s to mid 60s, which will be around 25 degrees above \nnormal. On Saturday, ridging aloft will break down. With the \nridge breakdown and a cold front dropping through the Black \nHills Saturday afternoon, very warm air will reside across \nwestern and north central Nebraska. Another very warm day is \nexpected across the forecast area. Southwesterly winds will be \nstrong at 20 to 35 mph across the east, and 15 to 25 mph across \nthe west. Near critical fire weather conditions are possible \nValentine through Imperial and areas to the west as afternoon \nhumidity as low as 15 to 20 percent combines with wind gust to \n25 mph. NBM forecast highs mainly 85 to 90. Highs could be a few\ndegrees warmer than this, supported by the latest MEX and ECX \nMOS guidance. As for records Saturday, they are 88, 93, 94 and \n95 degrees for Broken Bow, North Platte, Imperial and Valentine \nrespectively. \n\nThe cold front should progress through much of the area Saturday\nnight, with highs Sunday noticeably cooler with readings in the \nlower 60s in the Pine Ridge, to the upper 70s in far SW \nNebraska. Sunday night into Monday, the threat for light \nprecipitation will increase over northwestern Nebraska as mid \nlevel forcing tracks from Wyoming into the Dakotas. Monday and \nTuesday, highs have trended cooler from the mid to upper 50s \nnorth central to upper 60s far southwest Monday, and mainly the \nupper 50s Tuesday. Increasing chances for showers Monday night \nthrough Wednesday. Flow aloft will transition to southwesterly \nas a broad trough of low pressure deepens across the western \nU.S. This is expected to bring a slight warmup to the low to mid\n60s Wednesday, although confidence in the evolution of the \nupper trough remains low. Subtropical moisture is favored to be\ndrawn northward from east of the four corners into the Central \nPlains, when the best chances for showers arrive.",
